# The read-only system is mounted from the boot file's root hash (systemd-veritysetup-generator).
# Everything the office writes is on the data partition (encrypted, see crypttab), mounted in the
# initrd so it is there before anything starts. Nothing on it can be run as a program. /tmp is in
# memory; there is no swap.
# The machine-id lives on the data partition too: systemd won't follow a link for it, so the file is
# mounted over /etc/machine-id before systemd starts, and systemd writes it there on the first boot.
/dev/mapper/suveryn-data  /var  ext4   noexec,nosuid,nodev,x-initrd.mount  0 2
tmpfs                   /tmp  tmpfs  mode=1777,nosuid,nodev,size=25%     0 0
/var/lib/suveryn/host/machine-id  /etc/machine-id  none  bind,x-initrd.mount  0 0
